  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: S&EDisc 3
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "A new lab volunteer notices your research team working with human tumor
    cells and viruses that have been modified in the lab to study how potential cancer
    drugs might work. Curious, he asks another volunteer if these experiments had
    to be approved by the Institutional Biosafety Committee (IBC). The other volunteer
    explains, \u201CThe IBC has to approve the overall project and give safety guidelines
    when using certain materials, but they don\u2019t need to approve every single
    experiment in early discovery work.\u201D\n\nIs this true or false?"
  explanation: "In early discovery work, the IBC doesn\u2019t review every individual
    experiment. Instead, they approve the overall project and set the safety rules
    for working with materials like human cells and lab-modified viruses. Once the
    project is approved, researchers follow standard practices \u2014 using personal
    protective equipment (PPE), working in biosafety cabinets, and handling materials
    properly. As long as they follow these guidelines, they don\u2019t need separate
    approval for each experiment. "
  correctAnswer: True
  incorrectAnswer1: False
  incorrectAnswer2: 
  incorrectAnswer3: 
  questionRole: Safety & Ethics
  questionType: TrueFalse
  questionStage: Discovery
